                <pb facs="0748" n="652" sameAs="frus:frus1931-41v01/pg652"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102 Tientsin/289: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Japan</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Dooman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-06-22T14:00:00+09:00">June 22, 1939—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 22—3:33 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>290. Yesterday I was talking with the Director of the American Bureau, Japanese
                    Foreign Office, and stated to him that the Japanese would be making a great
                    mistake if they assumed that by not mistreating nationals of the United States
                    in Tientsin they would fail to arouse a strong reaction on the part of the
                    American public to the reports in the press of indignities, highly offensive to
                    our sense of decency, which are being inflicted on the British.</p>
                <p>Telegram sent to Peiping for repetition to Tientsin, Chungking, and Shanghai.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dooman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
